The block connects amount/extent and concentration rates, including dilution and open-boundary distinctions, with empirical kinetic laws and appropriate evidence. The reusable mathematical bridge defines ln through a reciprocal integral, proves its product identity and full real range, defines exp as its positive inverse and establishes inverse differentiability by a difference quotient. It then derives first-order initial-value solutions and uniqueness by an integrating factor, including zero concentration; zero- and second-order comparisons retain their dimensions and admissible domains. These are accessible foundations for the chemical account, not completion of mathematical analysis or differential equations.

The original reversible model fixes total concentration at 1 mmol/L, initial B at zero, forward/reverse constants at 3/4 and 1/4 min⁻¹ and fixed volume/temperature. It derives equilibrium B=3/4 mmol/L, ratio K=3 and relaxation rate 1 min⁻¹. At one and two half-relaxation times B is 3/8 and 9/16 mmol/L. A convex-combination argument proves admissibility. Rate scaling preserves the ratio and changes time; the identity between equilibrium ratio and rate-constant ratio is confined to the stipulated elementary ideal model. A separate open account balances unequal reaction fluxes to expose stationary nonequilibrium. Frozen kinetics is likewise distinguished from thermodynamic equilibrium.

The observation study uses exact affine response models. Equal sensitivities produce a constant 11/5 signal for every composition at the stipulated total. Distinct sensitivities give signals 39/20 and 93/40 at the two stated times and permit recovery only with the other inputs known. An unknown-baseline counterexample leaves composition ambiguous, while a small sensitivity difference amplifies response error. A separate uncorrected-baseline decay ratio shows why an apparently fitted kinetic constant can be biased. None of these synthetic examples establishes real kinetics, instrument calibration, selectivity or analytical method validation.

