Installed ten new lessons and reused three existing materials lessons after `@chemistry-identity-review`. The new sequence is `chemistry-phase-description`, `chemistry-phase-coexistence`, `chemistry-phase-correlation`, `chemistry-phase-amounts`, shared `mat-common-tangent`, `chemistry-phase-tangent`, shared `mat-regular-solution`, `chemistry-phase-stability`, shared `mat-regular-example`, `chemistry-phase-certificates`, `chemistry-phase-evidence`, `chemistry-phase-study`, `chemistry-phase-review`. Chemistry now has **107 common learning Records**. The three shared Records preserve their original prose, imports, identities, materials routes and solver; only bounded chemistry reading notes and common continuations were added. Existing general-chemistry common continuations remain unchanged. No new source identity was needed.

The block distinguishes phase/component/species, overall and phase composition, phase amount, intensive variance and allowed exchanges. It derives the nonreacting phase rule, pure-phase Clapeyron relation, its separate vapor/volume/enthalpy approximations, and a finite enthalpy bound. Conservation determines phase amounts after an admissible phase pair has been established; mole, mass and volume bases differ. The DeVoe equation 13.2.1 reconstruction gives 0.20 mol solid and 0.80 mol liquid from its stated 1 mol binary example. A separate hypothetical 4 mol mixture gives 3 and 1 mol, masses 72 and 36 g and equal stipulated phase volumes. These are source reconstruction and model calculations, not a performed experiment.

The NIST water reconstruction uses only the inspected 273–303 K Antoine row. At 298.15 K it gives approximately 0.03166749 bar; differentiating the fit and applying the ideal-vapor approximation yields apparent enthalpy 44.08731 kJ/mol. A constant-enthalpy model anchored there exceeds the original fit at 303 K by about 0.054193%. This compares two calculations derived from one correlation, not independent experimental validation. Parameter covariance, pointwise source uncertainty and stable-phase applicability are not invented from the displayed digits. Water and the hypothetical regular mixture retain separate material identities.

The common-tangent lesson derives both chemical potentials from extensive G and proves global minimality when a line supports every admissible candidate curve. Pairwise equality alone can fail in the presence of a lower third phase. The symmetric regular model is analyzed for both χ≤2 and χ>2: convexity, unique symmetric minima, the attained lower convex envelope, binodal/spinodal boundaries, metastability, activity coefficients and reference-line invariance. The model's assumptions and missing interface/kinetic terms remain explicit. A hypothetical third phase defeats a restricted phase-pair search. This supplies common chemical preparation for later branches rather than completing a phase-transformation or industrial-separation specialty.

The existing floating solver proposes compositions; the new program verifies them using the unchanged exact logarithm enclosure from the earlier entropy study. Opposite enclosed derivative signs plus positive curvature certify the unique left minimum, and symmetry supplies the right. For χ=3 the rational bracket [0.07072018,0.07072019] yields a minimum near −0.05834134944144. At overall composition 0.35 and total 10 mol, independently derived enclosures give left amount between 6.74711217 and 6.74711222 mol and right amount between 3.25288778 and 3.25288783 mol. The locally positive curvature at composition 0.1 does not prevent a lower separated state. The analytical supporting proof establishes global behavior; finite grid checks establish only their stated implementation comparisons.

The investigation includes an evidence plan for phase identity/composition and amounts, preparation/equilibration, kinetics and rival models; a changed-model or authorized-data extension; independent analysis, criticism and revision. It distinguishes numerical accuracy from empirical uncertainty and clouding from an equilibrium proof. No real material parameter, laboratory activity, observed learner performance or completed research project is claimed.

